{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q016_pdb_1acc_domain_split","answer":"What changed: In SCOP 1.75, PDB 1acc (anthrax protective antigen) chain A was one whole-chain domain, d1acca_ (sunid 43828, region "A:"). Its lineage was class f (membrane and cell surface proteins), fold/superfamily/family f.11.1.1 "Anthrax protective antigen", species Bacillus anthracis. Via the API, SCOPe 2.01–2.03 still show this single d1acca_ (f.11.1.1). Starting in SCOPe 2.04, and unchanged through 2.05, 2.06, 2.07 and the current 2.08, the chain is split into two domains. d1acca2 (sunid 238522, A:14-225) is b.179.1.1, class b (all beta), fold b.179 "Anthrax protective antigen N-terminal-like", superfamily b.179.1 "PA14-like", family/protein PA14. d1acca1 (sunid 238521, A:226-735) is f.60.1.1, class f, fold/superfamily/family f.60 "Anthrax protective antigen C-terminal-like". So the single 1.75 f.11 assignment was replaced by two domains in different classes (b and f). The old f.11 identifier no longer describes this chain, and the sids were renumbered from d1acca_ to d1acca1/d1acca2.

What it reveals: A multi-domain protein is not one evolutionary unit. Each SCOPe domain is a separate evolutionary module that can have its own ancestry, fold and relatives. Here the N-terminal segment is a PA14-like beta domain, a type of module that recurs in other proteins. The larger C-terminal segment is a different, mostly membrane-pore-related architecture. Whole-chain labels hide this. The split is a curation refinement: as more structures and relationships became known, one chain-level unit was divided into modules that each have their own homology evidence. It does not mean the protein's biology changed. Domain boundaries, and the fold and superfamily they receive, are release-dependent. Superfamily means probable common ancestry shown by structure and function. Fold is only structural similarity and does not by itself prove homology. Two domains from one chain can be homologous to completely unrelated protein families.

Effect on homology searches: (1) Do not treat the 1.75 f.11.1.1 label for 1acc as valid for the whole chain. Use the release-specific domain boundaries (226-735 for f.60.1.1 and 14-225 for b.179.1.1 in 2.04–2.08), and always record the SCOPe release and sid/sunid. (2) Split the query by domain before searching or building profiles or templates. A hit to the PA14-like region (b.179) supports homology and template use only for residues 14-225, not for the C-terminal f.60 region, and the reverse also holds. Whole-chain searches can produce spurious or partial hits, with alignment coverage covering only one module. (3) Use the superfamily or family level, not fold alone, to claim homology. Hits that share only a fold are not proof of common ancestry. (4) Function transfer is limited: sharing the PA14 domain does not imply the same toxin, binding or pore-forming function. Modular architectures (domain order and combination) are what to compare, and they need sequence-level and literature evidence, since SCOPe domain membership alone does not establish function. (5) When representative or non-redundant sets are chosen, pick representatives per domain (sid), not per PDB chain. Old benchmarks or annotations built on SCOP 1.75 (d1acca_, f.11) should be remapped to the newer domains, or the chain may be mis-binned or counted as one class when it spans two. This answer rests on the API's domain records and lineage. I did not verify the underlying structural basis for the split by independent structural analysis.","evidence_used":["/api/v1/releases","/api/v1/search?q=1acc","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1acc","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/1.75/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.01/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.02/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.03/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.04/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.05/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.06/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.07/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/r